          "title": "Cytokine-induced signaling pathway activity in bronchial epithelium",
          "omicsType": "transcriptomics",
          "omicsSubType": "bulk-rna-seq",
          "analytical_narrative": "The question asks which signaling pathways are ACTIVE in cytokine-stimulated vs vehicle-treated bronchial epithelium, judged by their transcriptional footprint rather than by member expression. That rules out a membership-only enrichment as the sole readout and calls for footprint-based pathway activity (PROGENy with decoupleR) as the primary analysis, complemented by Hallmark preranked GSEA on the same contrast and a signed regulon TF-activity pass (CollecTRI/DoRothEA via decoupleR ulm) to name upstream regulators. The data are 12,000 gene-level Salmon counts x 12 samples (6 treated vs 6 control, unpaired, single batch), so the DE layer is a DESeq2 Wald two-group model with median-of-ratios size factors, minimal pre-filtering, independent filtering at BH alpha 0.05, and apeglm shrinkage for reporting. Profiling flags sample_01 with a library size ~4x below the median: per the low-depth policy the sample is kept, inspected in QC (PCA, sample-distance heatmap, library size and detected genes), and down-weighted by its size factor rather than dropped silently; the DE fit is also reported with that sample excluded as a stated sensitivity analysis. Counts arrive as a gene-level table with no per-sample quant.sf or average-transcript-length table, so no length offset exists: the model runs on counts as delivered and the loss of length correction (isoform shifts can masquerade as DE) is stated in the report. The three activity readouts (pathway footprint, TF regulons, gene-set enrichment) are computed on the same DE contrast and converge in one synthesis.",
